<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<UserControl
    x:Class="Rudis.Shell.Regions.Chat"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    FontFamily="Calibri">

    <!--
      Chat — the region name is the handoff's, character-for-character
      (design_handoff_rudis_editor/README.md:36,142; CLAUDE.md rule 7).

      EVERY colour is a {StaticResource} token key from Theme/Tokens.xaml. A raw-hex
      literal in this file — INCLUDING IN A COMMENT — is a build FAILURE
      (MechanicalGatesTests.no_raw_hex_outside_the_token_dictionary).

      ═══ WHAT THIS REGION IS ═══

      The sixth instance of the established region pattern, over the FIVE agent
      exports that were already wrapped before this phase started
      (Interop/RudisNative.cs:203-230). There is NO new P/Invoke here and there is no
      new interop anywhere in this plan: a Chat step that seems to need one has
      misread the seam (Phase 53 D-11). `ApplyOptionCardAsync` gets its first caller
      in this file.

      It holds NO authoritative state (CLAUDE.md rule 4). The transcript is view
      state; every project mutation an agent turn makes arrives at the Timeline and
      MediaBin through the SAME mirror path every other command uses. There is
      deliberately no snapshot/poll call in this region — see Chat.xaml.cs.

      Every decision this file renders comes from the WinUI-FREE half beside it
      (Regions/Chat/ChatTurnPresenter.cs, plan 54-01), which is where the parity
      claims are asserted without a window.

      ═══ FIVE DELIBERATE DEVIATIONS FROM THE HANDOFF, ALL OWNER DECISIONS ═══

      Written here so none of them later reads as an oversight:

        1. NO `Chat.MinimizeButton`. The handoff names it an interactive atom
           (README:41,144) and README:164 keeps it out of the Esc rule. Both clauses
           existed because a FLOATING panel obscured content. D-01 docks Chat into
           the Canvas column, so it obscures nothing, and the Canvas/Chat splitter
           (D-02) is the size control the minimize button stood in for. DROPPED by
           D-04, deliberately.
        2. NO drag handle (U+2837) and no float chrome — D-01 voided the floating
           mechanics (draggable header, 300x332 default, clamp-on-resize, and the
           float shadow). This is a docked panel in a Grid row.
        3. Option cards render INSIDE this transcript (D-06), not in the Canvas.
           v6.0 resolved the handoff's own ambiguity the other way (README:98 lists
           cards under Canvas; README:147 puts them in Chat); this phase re-resolves
           it toward Chat, so README:98's Canvas-cards clause is deliberately
           unbuilt.
        4. NO streaming caret and NO structured tool-call trace. The handoff's
           States row lists a token-streaming caret; `rudis_agent_send_message` is
           ONE-SHOT, so there is nothing mid-turn to drive either (D-17).
        5. The status pill has TWO states, not three. v6.0's initial `connecting…`
           was markup that `refreshChatStatus` immediately replaced; the status read
           is a local keychain check, not a network call, so a third transient state
           would only flash. Disconnected is the honest pre-read state.

      ═══ THREE RENDERING RULES, ALL PREVIOUSLY PAID FOR (50-05) ═══

      · NO width-responsive visual-state trigger. Phase 50 MEASURED that the obvious
        XAML mechanism for one never fires in this shell; responsive is a measured
        SizeChanged, as Toolbar.xaml.cs's ApplyResponsiveLayout does. This region
        needs none at all — it stretches in both axes.
      · NO PUA icon-font codepoints. The two glyphs here are plain BMP Unicode,
        U+2726 (agent mark) and U+2191 (send) — and both were MEASURED rendered
        before they were committed. See the note at AgentMark.
      · NO `RadioButton` groups. If one ever lands here it needs
        `TabFocusNavigation="Once"` on its container, or each loose button in the
        group takes its own tab stop (the 50-05 trap).

      ═══ EVERY CONTROL HERE DRIVES SOMETHING REAL (rule 1) ═══

        Chat.SendButton  -> rudis_agent_send_message
        Chat.KeyButton   -> opens Settings (MainWindow.ShowSettingsAsync)
        the pill         <- rudis_agent_status
        Chat.AgentStatus <- rudis_agent_status (source -> AgentStatusText, D-69-15)
        a card's Apply   -> rudis_apply_option_card

      The inline BYO-key form (the old KeyForm with its KeyInput/KeySave/KeyClear/KeyCancel)
      was RETIRED in Phase 69 (D-69-10): the one key surface is Dialogs/SettingsDialog,
      so there are not two key surfaces that can drift.

      No button in this file ships with an inert handler: Task 2 of this same plan
      fills every one, which is why both halves landed in one plan.
    -->

    <UserControl.Resources>

        <!-- ═══ Input chrome ═══
             The shipped TextBox/PasswordBox templates paint themselves from WinUI
             SYSTEM brushes, which is raw hex by proxy and would make the rule-7 gate
             meaningless for this region. Retargeting the theme resource keys onto
             Rudis tokens is the smallest correct fix and follows the precedent plan
             53-02 set for GridViewItem: a resource lookup inside a control template
             walks the realized element's parent chain, and this dictionary sits on
             that chain above every control in the region — which is why the override
             lives here on the region rather than at app level, where it would have
             repainted every other input in the shell.

             The handoff's own stated use for `bg-elevated` is "Inputs, search", so
             the input fill is that token and the strip BEHIND it is `bg-bar`. -->
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackground" Color="{StaticResource bg-elevated-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackgroundPointerOver" Color="{StaticResource bg-elevated-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackgroundFocused" Color="{StaticResource bg-elevated-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackgroundDisabled" Color="{StaticResource bg-panel-color}" />

        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBorderBrush" Color="{StaticResource border-strong-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBorderBrushPointerOver" Color="{StaticResource border-strong-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBorderBrushFocused" Color="{StaticResource accent-bright-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBorderBrushDisabled" Color="{StaticResource border-hairline-color}" />

        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlForeground" Color="{StaticResource text-primary-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlForegroundPointerOver" Color="{StaticResource text-primary-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlForegroundFocused" Color="{StaticResource text-primary-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlForegroundDisabled" Color="{StaticResource text-tertiary-color}" />

        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlPlaceholderForeground" Color="{StaticResource text-tertiary-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlPlaceholderForegroundPointerOver" Color="{StaticResource text-tertiary-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlPlaceholderForegroundFocused" Color="{StaticResource text-tertiary-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlPlaceholderForegroundDisabled" Color="{StaticResource text-tertiary-color}" />

        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlSelectionHighlightColor" Color="{StaticResource accent-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlButtonBackground" Color="Transparent" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlButtonBackgroundPointerOver" Color="{StaticResource bg-segment-active-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlButtonForeground" Color="{StaticResource text-secondary-color}" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlButtonForegroundPointerOver" Color="{StaticResource text-primary-color}" />
        <Thickness x:Key="TextControlBorderThemeThickness">1</Thickness>
        <Thickness x:Key="TextControlBorderThemeThicknessFocused">1</Thickness>

        <!-- ═══ Brand fill: Chat.SendButton, a card's Apply ═══
             Handoff: fill `accent`, text `on-accent`; hover `accent-bright`. Same
             explicit token-only ControlTemplate every region since 50-05 uses, so
             nothing inherits a WinUI system brush. Disabled resolves as
             `bg-elevated` + `text-tertiary`, matching every other disabled control
             in the shell (the Toolbar's recorded UI-SPEC gap-4 resolution). -->
        <Style x:Key="ChatAccentButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource accent}" />
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource on-accent}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-control}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,0,10,0" />
            <Setter Property="Height" Value="26" />
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualPrimaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource accent-bright}" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualSecondaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource on-accent}" />
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Fill"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal" />
                                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource accent-bright}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource accent-bright}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-elevated}" />
                                            <Setter Target="Label.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-tertiary}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                </VisualStateGroup>
                            </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                              Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                                              FontSize="{TemplateBinding FontSize}"
                                              FontWeight="{TemplateBinding FontWeight}"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </Border>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ Flat/outlined: Chat.KeyButton ═══
             v6.0's `chat-key-btn` treatment, given the handoff's outlined-button
             chrome. 22px in the header band. -->
        <Style x:Key="ChatFlatButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-secondary}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource border-strong}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-control}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,0,8,0" />
            <Setter Property="Height" Value="22" />
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualPrimaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource accent-bright}" />
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualSecondaryBrush" Value="{StaticResource on-accent}" />
            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <Border x:Name="Fill"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                                <VisualStateGroup x:Name="CommonStates">
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Normal" />
                                    <VisualState x:Name="PointerOver">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-elevated}" />
                                            <Setter Target="Label.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-primary}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Pressed">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Fill.Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-segment-active}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                    <VisualState x:Name="Disabled">
                                        <VisualState.Setters>
                                            <Setter Target="Label.Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-tertiary}" />
                                        </VisualState.Setters>
                                    </VisualState>
                                </VisualStateGroup>
                            </VisualStateManager.VisualStateGroups>
                            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                                              Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                                              FontSize="{TemplateBinding FontSize}"
                                              FontWeight="{TemplateBinding FontWeight}"
                                              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </Border>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ Transcript bubbles ═══
             Built IMPERATIVELY by Chat.xaml.cs's AppendBubble (v6.0's
             appendChatMessage idiom): an ItemsControl with a template selector buys
             nothing here, and the imperative path keeps the "TextBlock.Text only,
             never a markup path" rule (T-54-04) visible at its single call site.
             The four kinds are v6.0's four CSS classes, one style pair each.

             ⚠ ERROR HAS NO RED. The handoff's colour table defines no error/danger
             token — v6.0's red was raw hex in a token-free shell. Inventing one
             would break rule 7 the same way inventing the disconnected pill's amber
             would (see Tokens.xaml). An error bubble is therefore `bg-elevated` with
             a `border-strong` outline against the borderless agent bubble, and the
             KIND is carried losslessly for UIA by the bubble's AutomationId prefix
             (`Chat.ErrorBubble.{n}`), which is what plan 54-05's proof reads. -->
        <Style x:Key="ChatBubbleUser" TargetType="Border">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource accent}" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-panel}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,5,8,5" />
            <Setter Property="MaxWidth" Value="320" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="ChatBubbleAgent" TargetType="Border">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-elevated}" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-panel}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,5,8,5" />
            <Setter Property="MaxWidth" Value="320" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="ChatBubbleDisclosure" TargetType="Border">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource accent-tint}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource border-strong}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-panel}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,5,8,5" />
            <Setter Property="MaxWidth" Value="320" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="ChatBubbleError" TargetType="Border">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource bg-elevated}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource border-strong}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-panel}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,5,8,5" />
            <Setter Property="MaxWidth" Value="320" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="ChatBubbleTextOnAccent" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource on-accent}" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
            <Setter Property="IsTextSelectionEnabled" Value="True" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="ChatBubbleText" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-primary}" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
            <Setter Property="IsTextSelectionEnabled" Value="True" />
        </Style>

        <!-- Consolas signals "literal / verbatim quoted text", which is exactly what a
             generation disclosure is: the prompt actually sent, the frames that
             conditioned it, the model that ran, and the standing provider notice —
             each on a STRUCTURAL channel because narration is known to paraphrase.
             v6.0 used a monospace stack here for the same reason. -->
        <Style x:Key="ChatBubbleTextDisclosure" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-secondary}" />
            <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Consolas" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
            <Setter Property="IsTextSelectionEnabled" Value="True" />
        </Style>

        <!-- ═══ Option card (D-06: in this transcript, not the Canvas) ═══
             Built imperatively by RenderCards, styled from here. `accent-tint` is
             the handoff's own "AI/agent surfaces (avatars, suggestion card)" token
             and the U+2726 mark in `accent-glyph` is its "✦ chip" guidance
             (README:104) — a card must read as MODEL OUTPUT, not as chrome. -->
        <Style x:Key="ChatOptionCard" TargetType="Border">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource accent-tint}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource border-strong}" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource radius-panel}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="9,7,9,8" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="ChatOptionCardMark" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource accent-glyph}" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="ChatOptionCardLabel" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-primary}" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
            <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="ChatOptionCardRationale" TargetType="TextBlock">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource text-secondary}" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
        </Style>

    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!-- The region's own edge against the Canvas above it. Under D-01 the Canvas row
         splits and Chat takes the bottom half, so this hairline IS the seam the
         splitter (plan 54-05) sits on. -->
    <Border Background="{StaticResource bg-panel}"
            BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-hairline}"
            BorderThickness="0,1,0,0">
        <Grid x:Name="ChatRoot">
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                <RowDefinition Height="*" />
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <!-- ═══ Row 0 — the header band ═══
                 36px to match the MediaBin header directly below it in the same
                 column, so the two bands read as one vocabulary (README:66). -->
            <Border Grid.Row="0"
                    Height="36"
                    Background="{StaticResource bg-bar}"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-hairline}"
                    BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
                    Padding="11,0,11,0"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.Header"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="Chat header">
                <Grid VerticalAlignment="Center">

                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                                Spacing="6"
                                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center">

                        <!-- The handoff's AI mark (README:144).

                             ⚠ MEASURED 2026-07-31, recorded because Phase 50 was
                             bitten TWICE by glyph rendering and the obvious fix here
                             would have been the wrong one. U+2726 is a BMP
                             pictograph, so the 50-05 trap to fear is not tofu (that
                             is the PUA failure) but a silent DirectWrite fallback to
                             Segoe UI Emoji, which would render it as a COLOUR emoji
                             in a monochrome UI — exactly what happened to the U+270E
                             pencil in 50-05.

                             It was rendered and LOOKED AT before being committed:
                             U+2726 and U+2191 were drawn at 34px in three families
                             (Calibri / SymbolThemeFontFamily / control default) and
                             captured to a bitmap. ALL THREE render MONOCHROME, with
                             no colour fallback and no tofu, so the inherited
                             Calibri needs NO family swap and NO ThemeResource
                             override — the simplest markup is also the correct one.
                             Transcript: artifacts/54-03-winui-mechanics.md. -->
                        <TextBlock x:Name="AgentMark"
                                   Text="&#x2726;"
                                   Foreground="{StaticResource accent-glyph}"
                                   FontSize="13"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                        <TextBlock x:Name="RegionTag"
                                   Text="Rudis Agent"
                                   Foreground="{StaticResource text-secondary}"
                                   FontSize="11"
                                   FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   CharacterSpacing="40"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.RegionTag"
                                   AutomationProperties.Name="Rudis Agent" />
                    </StackPanel>

                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                                Spacing="6"
                                HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center">

                        <!-- Chat.StatusPill — driven by rudis_agent_status'
                             {key_configured} (D-19).

                             CONNECTED uses the handoff's own `success` / `success-bg`
                             pair, whose stated uses are literally "Connected status
                             text" and "Connected status pill".

                             DISCONNECTED uses the EXISTING neutral trio
                             (`bg-elevated` / `border-strong` / `text-secondary`).
                             The handoff calls this state amber but defines no
                             amber/warning token anywhere in its colour table, and
                             v6.0's amber was a raw-hex approximation in a token-free
                             shell. Adding a token the handoff does not have would
                             violate rule 7 in the other direction — the named tokens
                             ARE the contract. Recorded here and in Tokens.xaml.

                             Both colour sets and the text are applied by
                             ApplyStatus, so the pill and its UIA Name can never
                             disagree about which state is on screen. -->
                        <Border x:Name="StatusPill"
                                Background="{StaticResource bg-elevated}"
                                BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-strong}"
                                BorderThickness="1"
                                CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-control}"
                                Padding="7,1,7,1"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.StatusPill"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="disconnected">
                            <TextBlock x:Name="StatusPillText"
                                       Text="DISCONNECTED"
                                       Foreground="{StaticResource text-secondary}"
                                       FontSize="10"
                                       FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                       CharacterSpacing="50" />
                        </Border>

                        <!-- Chat.AgentStatus (Phase 69, D-69-15) - the status TEXT a proof
                             reads: exactly `connected`, `connected (from environment
                             variable - developer setup)` or `unavailable - no API key - add
                             one in Settings`, from AgentStatusText.Describe. The proofs read
                             the UIA Name (full text); the visible trimming is decoration.
                             The pill's Name stays `connected`/`disconnected` so prior gates
                             do not churn, and ApplyStatus sets both from one parse. -->
                        <TextBlock x:Name="AgentStatusLabel"
                                   Text="unavailable — no API key — add one in Settings"
                                   Foreground="{StaticResource text-tertiary}"
                                   FontSize="10"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   MaxWidth="180"
                                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.AgentStatus"
                                   AutomationProperties.Name="unavailable — no API key — add one in Settings"
                                   ToolTipService.ToolTip="unavailable — no API key — add one in Settings" />

                        <!-- Content is set by ApplyStatus: `Change key` when a key is
                             configured, `Reconnect` when it is not (v6.0's exact two
                             labels). It starts at `Reconnect` because that is the
                             pre-read state the pill also shows. -->
                        <Button x:Name="KeyButton"
                                Style="{StaticResource ChatFlatButtonStyle}"
                                Content="Reconnect"
                                Click="OnKeyButtonClick"
                                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.KeyButton"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="Reconnect"
                                ToolTipService.ToolTip="Open Settings to connect or change your Anthropic API key" />

                        <!-- The handoff's minimize atom would sit HERE, and is
                             deliberately absent: deviation 1 in this file's header
                             names it in full and records D-04's reasoning. It is
                             written down rather than merely skipped, so a
                             handoff-conformance review reads a decision rather than
                             an omission. -->

                        <!-- No drag handle either (deviation 2) — this is a docked
                             panel in a Grid row, not a floating card. -->
                    </StackPanel>
                </Grid>
            </Border>

            <!-- ═══ Row 1 — the transcript, with the empty state overlaid ═══ -->
            <Grid Grid.Row="1">

                <ScrollViewer x:Name="TranscriptScroll"
                              VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                              HorizontalScrollMode="Disabled"
                              HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                    <StackPanel x:Name="TranscriptPanel"
                                Spacing="6"
                                Padding="8"
                                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.Transcript"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="Chat transcript" />
                </ScrollViewer>

                <!-- The handoff gives this copy verbatim (README:144): a centered
                     U+2726 above the hint. Its reference to sketching on the canvas
                     is still TRUE under D-01 — the Canvas sits directly above this
                     region, sharing the column. The AutomationProperties.Name is the
                     same string because plan 54-05's UIA proof reads it verbatim. -->
                <StackPanel x:Name="EmptyState"
                            Spacing="8"
                            Padding="24,0,24,0"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.EmptyState"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Ask Rudis to edit your video, or sketch an idea on the canvas.">
                    <TextBlock Text="&#x2726;"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource accent-glyph}"
                               FontSize="20"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Center" />
                    <TextBlock Text="Ask Rudis to edit your video, or sketch an idea on the canvas."
                               Foreground="{StaticResource text-tertiary}"
                               FontSize="12"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               TextAlignment="Center" />
                </StackPanel>
            </Grid>

            <!-- ═══ Row 2 — Chat › Composer ═══ -->
            <Border Grid.Row="2"
                    Background="{StaticResource bg-bar}"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-hairline}"
                    BorderThickness="0,1,0,0"
                    Padding="8">
                <Grid ColumnSpacing="6">
                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                    <!-- ⚠ PreviewKeyDown, NOT KeyDown — MEASURED, and the obvious
                         answer is the WRONG one. A multiline TextBox marks Enter
                         HANDLED before any instance KeyDown handler runs, so a
                         `KeyDown="..."` handler never fires for Enter at all and the
                         newline lands regardless. Full transcript and the four other
                         cases in Chat.xaml.cs's OnComposerPreviewKeyDown. -->
                    <TextBox x:Name="ComposerInput"
                             Grid.Column="0"
                             AcceptsReturn="True"
                             TextWrapping="Wrap"
                             MaxHeight="96"
                             PlaceholderText="Ask Rudis or sketch&#x2026;"
                             FontSize="12"
                             CornerRadius="{StaticResource radius-control}"
                             PreviewKeyDown="OnComposerPreviewKeyDown"
                             AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.Composer"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="Message Rudis" />

                    <!-- Chat.SendButton is a HANDOFF NAME (README:41,144) — keep it
                         exact. U+2191, measured monochrome; see AgentMark above. -->
                    <Button x:Name="SendButton"
                            Grid.Column="1"
                            Style="{StaticResource ChatAccentButtonStyle}"
                            Content="&#x2191;"
                            FontSize="14"
                            MinWidth="32"
                            VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                            Click="OnSendClick"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.SendButton"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Send"
                            ToolTipService.ToolTip="Send (Enter; Shift+Enter for a new line)" />
                </Grid>
            </Border>
        </Grid>
    </Border>
</UserControl>
